      "seed": 1644086440,
      "version": 194,
      "versionNonce": 952580259,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790061545259,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Request\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": 789,\n      \"canonicalPostId\": 123,\n      \"authorId\": 5,\n      \"author\": {\n        \"userId\": 5,\n        \"displayName\": \"Ahmed Ali\",\n        \"profileImageKey\": \"users/5/avatar.jpg\",\n        \"headline\": \"Backend Developer\"\n      },\n      \"content\": null,\n      \"visibility\": 1,\n      \"isPureRepost\": true,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": 123,\n      \"repostOf\": {\n        \"id\": 123,\n        \"authorId\": 7,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"userId\": 7,\n          \"displayName\": \"Mohamed Hassan\",\n          \"profileImageKey\": \"users/7/avatar.jpg\",\n          \"headline\": \"Product Manager\"\n        },\n        \"content\": \"Hello LinkedIn #dotnet\",\n        \"media\": [\n          { \"objectKey\": \"posts/123/img1.jpg\", \"mediaType\": 1, \"displayOrder\": 0 }\n        ],\n        \"mentions\": [],\n        \"hashtags\": [\"dotnet\"],\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T10:00:00Z\"\n      },\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": false,\n      \"media\": [],\n      \"mentions\": [],\n      \"hashtags\": [],\n      \"counters\": {\n        \"reactions\": 150,\n        \"comments\": 20,\n        \"reposts\": 7\n      },\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T16:00:00Z\",\n      \"updatedAt\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": 788,\n      \"canonicalPostId\": 788,\n      \"authorId\": 5,\n      \"author\": { ... Ahmed ... },\n      \"content\": \"Just shipped a new feature! #dotnet\",\n      \"visibility\": 1,\n      \"isPureRepost\": false,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": null,\n      \"repostOf\": null,\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": false,\n      \"media\": [...],\n      \"mentions\": [],\n      \"hashtags\": [\"dotnet\"],\n      \"counters\": {\n        \"reactions\": 42,\n        \"comments\": 5,\n        \"reposts\": 2\n      },\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T15:00:00Z\",\n      \"updatedAt\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nextCursor\": \"NjM4NjIyMjIyMDAwMDAwMDAwOjc4OA==\",\n  \"hasMore\": true\n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Request\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": 789,\n      \"canonicalPostId\": 123,\n      \"authorId\": 5,\n      \"author\": {\n        \"userId\": 5,\n        \"displayName\": \"Ahmed Ali\",\n        \"profileImageKey\": \"users/5/avatar.jpg\",\n        \"headline\": \"Backend Developer\"\n      },\n      \"content\": null,\n      \"visibility\": 1,\n      \"isPureRepost\": true,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": 123,\n      \"repostOf\": {\n        \"id\": 123,\n        \"authorId\": 7,\n        \"author\": {\n          \"userId\": 7,\n          \"displayName\": \"Mohamed Hassan\",\n          \"profileImageKey\": \"users/7/avatar.jpg\",\n          \"headline\": \"Product Manager\"\n        },\n        \"content\": \"Hello LinkedIn #dotnet\",\n        \"media\": [\n          { \"objectKey\": \"posts/123/img1.jpg\", \"mediaType\": 1, \"displayOrder\": 0 }\n        ],\n        \"mentions\": [],\n        \"hashtags\": [\"dotnet\"],\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T10:00:00Z\"\n      },\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": false,\n      \"media\": [],\n      \"mentions\": [],\n      \"hashtags\": [],\n      \"counters\": {\n        \"reactions\": 150,\n        \"comments\": 20,\n        \"reposts\": 7\n      },\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T16:00:00Z\",\n      \"updatedAt\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": 788,\n      \"canonicalPostId\": 788,\n      \"authorId\": 5,\n      \"author\": { ... Ahmed ... },\n      \"content\": \"Just shipped a new feature! #dotnet\",\n      \"visibility\": 1,\n      \"isPureRepost\": false,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": null,\n      \"repostOf\": null,\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": false,\n      \"media\": [...],\n      \"mentions\": [],\n      \"hashtags\": [\"dotnet\"],\n      \"counters\": {\n        \"reactions\": 42,\n        \"comments\": 5,\n        \"reposts\": 2\n      },\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-20T15:00:00Z\",\n      \"updatedAt\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nextCursor\": \"NjM4NjIyMjIyMDAwMDAwMDAwOjc4OA==\",\n  \"hasMore\": true\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "V8jYE5-aHWm4lMyTufxEX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4345.693783145839,
      "y": 87.17335340176146,
      "width": 1661.5592041015625,
      "height": 3850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1288475501,
      "version": 51,
      "versionNonce": 1692250477,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790060881706,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "EC1: AuthorId does not exist in the system\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n    \n    Why? The PostService does not know which users exist. The BFF handles the \"User not found\" response from the UserService.\n    \n    Rejected alternative: 404 Not Found — because we would need an extra query to check UserSummaries. Also, even if the user exists, they might simply have no posts.\n\nEC2: Author has 0 posts\n\n    Behavior:\n    \n    {\n      \"Items\": [],\n      \"HasMore\": false,\n      \"NextCursor\": null\n    }\nEC3: Author has only private posts and I am not the author\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n\nEC4: Invalid cursor (bad Base64)\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \n    The Validator handles this.\n\nEC5: Cursor points to a deleted post\n\n    Behavior: The WHERE clause ignores deleted posts, so the cursor simply skips it.\n    \n    The result is still correct.\n\nEC6: Cursor is older than the oldest available post\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n\n    There are no more posts after the cursor.\n    \nEC7: Pure repost + original post is deleted\n\n    Behavior:\n    \n    {\n      \"id\": 789,\n      \"isPureRepost\": true,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": 123,\n      \"repostOf\": null,\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": true\n    }\n    \n    The Frontend shows:\n    \n    This post is no longer available.\n    \nEC8: A post has many media files (9)\n\n    Behavior: Return all media files.\n    \n    We do not set a limit on the number of media files.\n    \n    If the response becomes too large, we can limit the number of media files per post in the future.\n    \nEC9: User makes 1000 reposts in one day\n\n    Behavior: Pagination handles them normally.\n    \n    Each page contains 20 posts.\n    \n    Since all 1000 reposts may have almost the same CreatedAt, Id DESC is used as a tie-breaker to keep the ordering stable.\n\nEC10: Deleted post exists inside the cursor range\n\n    Behavior: The query ignores the deleted post.\n    \n    Example:\n    \n    Posts: 100, 99, 98 (deleted), 97, 96, ...\n    Cursor = 99\n    \n    Next page:\n    WHERE Id < 99\n    → 98, 97, 96, ...\n    \n    98 is deleted, so it is removed.\n    \n    Result:\n    [97, 96, ...]\n    \n    The actual page may contain fewer posts than the requested limit.\n    \n    This is okay because HasMore tells us whether there are more posts.\n\nEC11: Author is the current user and has private posts\n\n    Behavior: All posts are returned, including private posts.\n\nEC12: Author is deleted from UserService (DeletedAt)\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Posts are returned normally.\n\n    Why? The PostService does not know that the user was deleted.\n    \n    The UserSummary may still exist or may be removed separately through an event.\n\n    The BFF handles this and can show:\n    \n    This user account was deleted.\n\nEC13: Same author has many pure reposts of the same original post\n\n    Example:\n    \n    Ahmed reposted Mohamed's post 5 times, if the system allows it.\n    \n    Behavior: All 5 reposts are returned.\n    \n    Note: The CreatePostHandler should normally prevent this, similar to LinkedIn. But this is outside the scope of this query.\n\nEC14: Repost of a pure repost (repost chain)\n\n    In theory, this should not be allowed during post creation.\n    \n    If it happens, RepostOf returns the pure repost itself, not the original post.\n    \n    Protection: In CreatePost, reject RepostOfPostId if the target post is already a pure repost.\n\nEC15: Limit is greater than the maximum allowed value\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \n    The Validator handles this.\n    \n    Rejected alternative: Automatically change the limit to 50.\n    \n    This is rejected because silently changing the user's request can be confusing.\n\nEC16: Limit = 0\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \nEC17: AuthorId is the current user\n\n    Behavior: Private posts are also returned.\n    \n    The Frontend shows an \"Only you\" badge on each private post.\n\nEC18: Very small page size (limit = 1)\n\n    Behavior: Works normally.\n    \n    NextCursor points to the only post returned.\n    \n    Performance: This can cause more queries because there are more pages, but each query is fast, so it is not a major problem.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "EC1: AuthorId does not exist in the system\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n    \n    Why? The PostService does not know which users exist. The BFF handles the \"User not found\" response from the UserService.\n    \n    Rejected alternative: 404 Not Found — because we would need an extra query to check UserSummaries. Also, even if the user exists, they might simply have no posts.\n\nEC2: Author has 0 posts\n\n    Behavior:\n    \n    {\n      \"Items\": [],\n      \"HasMore\": false,\n      \"NextCursor\": null\n    }\nEC3: Author has only private posts and I am not the author\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n\nEC4: Invalid cursor (bad Base64)\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \n    The Validator handles this.\n\nEC5: Cursor points to a deleted post\n\n    Behavior: The WHERE clause ignores deleted posts, so the cursor simply skips it.\n    \n    The result is still correct.\n\nEC6: Cursor is older than the oldest available post\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Items: []\n\n    There are no more posts after the cursor.\n    \nEC7: Pure repost + original post is deleted\n\n    Behavior:\n    \n    {\n      \"id\": 789,\n      \"isPureRepost\": true,\n      \"repostOfPostId\": 123,\n      \"repostOf\": null,\n      \"isRepostOfUnavailable\": true\n    }\n    \n    The Frontend shows:\n    \n    This post is no longer available.\n    \nEC8: A post has many media files (9)\n\n    Behavior: Return all media files.\n    \n    We do not set a limit on the number of media files.\n    \n    If the response becomes too large, we can limit the number of media files per post in the future.\n    \nEC9: User makes 1000 reposts in one day\n\n    Behavior: Pagination handles them normally.\n    \n    Each page contains 20 posts.\n    \n    Since all 1000 reposts may have almost the same CreatedAt, Id DESC is used as a tie-breaker to keep the ordering stable.\n\nEC10: Deleted post exists inside the cursor range\n\n    Behavior: The query ignores the deleted post.\n    \n    Example:\n    \n    Posts: 100, 99, 98 (deleted), 97, 96, ...\n    Cursor = 99\n    \n    Next page:\n    WHERE Id < 99\n    → 98, 97, 96, ...\n    \n    98 is deleted, so it is removed.\n    \n    Result:\n    [97, 96, ...]\n    \n    The actual page may contain fewer posts than the requested limit.\n    \n    This is okay because HasMore tells us whether there are more posts.\n\nEC11: Author is the current user and has private posts\n\n    Behavior: All posts are returned, including private posts.\n\nEC12: Author is deleted from UserService (DeletedAt)\n\n    Behavior: 200 OK + Posts are returned normally.\n\n    Why? The PostService does not know that the user was deleted.\n    \n    The UserSummary may still exist or may be removed separately through an event.\n\n    The BFF handles this and can show:\n    \n    This user account was deleted.\n\nEC13: Same author has many pure reposts of the same original post\n\n    Example:\n    \n    Ahmed reposted Mohamed's post 5 times, if the system allows it.\n    \n    Behavior: All 5 reposts are returned.\n    \n    Note: The CreatePostHandler should normally prevent this, similar to LinkedIn. But this is outside the scope of this query.\n\nEC14: Repost of a pure repost (repost chain)\n\n    In theory, this should not be allowed during post creation.\n    \n    If it happens, RepostOf returns the pure repost itself, not the original post.\n    \n    Protection: In CreatePost, reject RepostOfPostId if the target post is already a pure repost.\n\nEC15: Limit is greater than the maximum allowed value\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \n    The Validator handles this.\n    \n    Rejected alternative: Automatically change the limit to 50.\n    \n    This is rejected because silently changing the user's request can be confusing.\n\nEC16: Limit = 0\n\n    Behavior: 400 Bad Request\n    \nEC17: AuthorId is the current user\n\n    Behavior: Private posts are also returned.\n    \n    The Frontend shows an \"Only you\" badge on each private post.\n\nEC18: Very small page size (limit = 1)\n\n    Behavior: Works normally.\n    \n    NextCursor points to the only post returned.\n    \n    Performance: This can cause more queries because there are more pages, but each query is fast, so it is not a major problem.",
